---
layout: article
title: Endpoint checks
description: Check HTTP, DNS, TCP, and SMTP endpoints on a schedule with Appwrite Cloud synthetic checks, and set the rules that open an alert.
---

Endpoint checks, called synthetic checks in the Console, are available on paid Appwrite Cloud plans. Appwrite connects to your target on a schedule and opens an alert when the check fails.

Synthetic checks count toward one allowance for the whole organization, across all of its projects. Pro includes 10 checks, and each additional check costs $2 per month. The shortest check interval is 1 minute on Pro and 30 seconds on Enterprise. Synthetic checks aren't available on the Free plan, and an organization can't move to Free while it has any. See [Pricing](/pricing) for details.

# Create an endpoint check {% #create-an-endpoint-check %}

1. In your project, open **Observe** > **Monitors**, then click **Create monitor**.
2. Choose **HTTP**, **DNS**, **TCP**, or **SMTP** as the type. Under **Target**, enter what to check and what a healthy response looks like.
3. Under **Checks**, set how often to check and how long a check can take before it times out. Checks run from every 30 seconds to every hour, and your plan sets the shortest interval. The timeout ranges from 5 seconds to 1 minute and can't be longer than the interval.
4. Choose recipients under **Notify** and pick a **Severity**. To set a name or change the cooldown, expand **Name, cooldown**.
5. Click **Create**.

{% only_dark %}
![Create monitor page with an HTTP check of api.example.com/health that expects status 200 and warns 14 days before the certificate expires](/images/docs/monitors/dark/http-target.avif)
{% /only_dark %}
{% only_light %}
![Create monitor page with an HTTP check of api.example.com/health that expects status 200 and warns 14 days before the certificate expires](/images/docs/monitors/http-target.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The **Monitors** list shows the latest result, latency, and time of each check. Open a monitor to see its status, target, latest result, and when it last fired.

Some failures always open an alert, whatever you expect from the target: a timeout, a domain that doesn't resolve, a refused or failed connection, a failed TLS handshake, or a target in a private network, which Appwrite doesn't connect to.

# HTTP {% #http %}

Enter an `http://` or `https://` URL and choose a request method. You can add headers and, for methods that send one, a request body. Appwrite follows redirects and ignores the response body.

Enter a comma-separated list of **Expected status codes**, or leave it empty to accept any 2xx response.

For an HTTPS URL, **Certificate expiry warning** sets how many days before the certificate expires the check fails. It defaults to 14 days. So that certificates renewed on a normal schedule don't fire, the warning also waits until less than a quarter of the certificate's lifetime is left. Set it to `0` to turn the warning off. An invalid certificate still fails the check.

# DNS {% #dns %}

Enter the domain and select an **A**, **AAAA**, **CNAME**, **MX**, **TXT**, or **NS** record. Enter an **Expected answer** to fail when the first record doesn't match it, or leave it empty to fail only when the domain returns no records. Appwrite compares answers in lowercase, without the trailing dot.

**Resolver** takes an IP address with an optional port, such as `1.1.1.1` or `[2606:4700:4700::1111]:53`. Leave it empty to use Appwrite's resolver.

# TCP {% #tcp %}

Enter a host and port. The check passes when Appwrite can open a TCP connection to that port within the timeout, and it closes the connection straight away. Use an HTTP or SMTP check when you also need to verify how the service responds.

# SMTP {% #smtp %}

Enter the mail server's host and port. Appwrite reads the server's greeting, sends `EHLO`, and secures the connection as the **TLS** setting asks. It never signs in or sends mail.

| TLS setting | Behavior |
| --- | --- |
| **From the port** | Implicit TLS on port 465, STARTTLS on port 587, and a plain connection on other ports. |
| **None** | Connect without TLS. |
| **Upgrade with STARTTLS** | Upgrade the session with STARTTLS. The check fails if the server doesn't offer it. |
| **Implicit TLS** | Start TLS as soon as the connection opens. |

# Create with a server SDK {% #create-with-a-server-sdk %}

Each type has its own method: `createHttpMonitor`, `createDnsMonitor`, `createTcpMonitor`, and `createSmtpMonitor`. Create an [API key](/docs/partners/project/api-keys) with the `monitors.write` scope to call them. This example checks `https://api.example.com/health` every minute and opens a **Critical** alert when the response isn't a 2xx status or takes longer than 2 seconds.

{% multicode %}
```server-nodejs
const sdk = require('node-appwrite');

const client = new sdk.Client()
    .setEndpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
    .setProject('<PROJECT_ID>')
    .setKey('<API_KEY>');

const monitors = new sdk.Monitors(client);

const monitor = await monitors.createHttpMonitor({
    monitorId: 'api-health',
    name: 'API health',
    url: 'https://api.example.com/health',
    rules: [
        sdk.Query.or([
            sdk.Query.notBetween('statusCode', 200, 299),
            sdk.Query.greaterThan('latency', 2000)
        ])
    ], // optional
    interval: 60, // optional
    timeout: 10, // optional
    severity: sdk.MonitorSeverity.Critical // optional
});
```

```server-python
from appwrite.client import Client
from appwrite.services.monitors import Monitors
from appwrite.query import Query
from appwrite.enums.monitor_severity import MonitorSeverity

client = Client()
client.set_endpoint('https://<REGION>.cloud.appwrite.io/v1')
client.set_project('<PROJECT_ID>')
client.set_key('<API_KEY>')

monitors = Monitors(client)

monitor = monitors.create_http_monitor(
    monitor_id = 'api-health',
    name = 'API health',
    url = 'https://api.example.com/health',
    rules = [
        Query.or_queries([
            Query.not_between('statusCode', 200, 299),
            Query.greater_than('latency', 2000)
        ])
    ], # optional
    interval = 60, # optional
    timeout = 10, # optional
    severity = MonitorSeverity.CRITICAL # optional
)
```

