Content-Type: application/json
X-Appwrite-Project: <PROJECT_ID>
X-Appwrite-Key: <API_KEY>

{
  "monitorId": "api-health",
  "name": "API health",
  "url": "https://api.example.com/health",
  "rules": ["{\"method\":\"or\",\"values\":[{\"method\":\"notBetween\",\"attribute\":\"statusCode\",\"values\":[200,299]},{\"method\":\"greaterThan\",\"attribute\":\"latency\",\"values\":[2000]}]}"],
  "interval": 60,
  "timeout": 10,
  "severity": "critical"
}
```
{% /multicode %}

Each type takes its own target parameters:

| Method | Target parameters |
| --- | --- |
| `createHttpMonitor` | `url`, plus optional `method`, `headers`, and `body`. `method` defaults to `GET` and also takes `HEAD`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`, or `OPTIONS`. `body` takes up to 16,384 characters. |
| `createDnsMonitor` | `domain`, plus optional `record` and `resolver`. `record` defaults to `A` and also takes `AAAA`, `CNAME`, `MX`, `TXT`, or `NS`. |
| `createTcpMonitor` | `host` and `port` |
| `createSmtpMonitor` | `host` and `port`, plus optional `tlsMode`. It defaults to `auto`, which chooses from the port, and also takes `none`, `starttls`, or `implicit`. |

All four also take these parameters:

| Parameter | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `rules` | Queries that describe a failing check. See [Rules](#rules). |
| `interval` | Seconds between checks. Defaults to 60, or to your plan's shortest interval when that's longer. |
| `timeout` | Seconds a check can take, from 1 to 60 and no longer than `interval`. Defaults to 10. |
| `cooldown`, `enabled`, `notify`, `severity`, `escalationId` | The same as for [usage monitors](/docs/products/monitors/usage#create-with-a-server-sdk). |

Each type has a matching update method, such as `updateHttpMonitor`, which only changes the parameters you pass.

# Rules {% #rules %}

Rules describe a failing check: the monitor opens an alert when they match. Every top-level rule must match. Wrap rules in `Query.or()` to fire when any of them matches, and use `Query.and()` to group rules inside it. Rules take the `equal`, `notEqual`, `lessThan`, `lessThanEqual`, `greaterThan`, `greaterThanEqual`, `between`, and `notBetween` queries.

| Type | Attributes | Fails by default on |
| --- | --- | --- |
| HTTP | `statusCode`, `latency` in milliseconds, `certificateDays` left, and `certificateLifetime`, the percentage of the certificate's lifetime left | A status outside 200 to 299, or a certificate with fewer than 14 days and less than 25% of its lifetime left |
| DNS | `answer`, the first record in lowercase without the trailing dot, which only takes `equal` and `notEqual`; `answers`, the number of records; and `latency` | No records |
| TCP | `latency` | Connection failures only |
| SMTP | `code`, the server's reply code, plus `latency`, `certificateDays`, and `certificateLifetime` | Connection failures only |

Your rules replace the defaults, so add certificate rules to an HTTP check if you still want it to warn before the certificate expires. Passing `rules` to an update method replaces all of the monitor's rules. The failures listed under [Create an endpoint check](#create-an-endpoint-check) open an alert whatever the rules say.

The Console saves **Expected status codes**, **Certificate expiry warning**, and **Expected answer** as rules. It keeps rules set through the API that those fields can't show until you change one of the fields.

